# IncidentInsight environment template.
#
# The application reads these settings DIRECTLY from the process environment
# (os.environ). It does NOT load a `.env` file automatically - there is no
# dotenv dependency in this project, so copying this template to `.env` would
# have no effect. Set the variables in your shell instead:
#
# Windows (Command Prompt):
#   set OPENAI_API_KEY=your-key-here
#   set OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini
#
# Windows (PowerShell):
#   $env:OPENAI_API_KEY = "your-key-here"
#   $env:OPENAI_MODEL   = "gpt-4o-mini"
#
# macOS / Linux:
#   export OPENAI_API_KEY="your-key-here"
#   export OPENAI_MODEL="gpt-4o-mini"
#
# These apply to the current shell session only. Put the set/export line in your
# shell profile (or your IDE's run configuration) to make them permanent. Never
# commit a real key, and never paste one into the web UI.
#
# BOTH SETTINGS ARE OPTIONAL. Without an API key the whole application still
# works: parsing, clustering, ranking, the deterministic explanations, the
# dashboard and the chatbot's offline fallback all run locally with no network
# access. A key is only needed for the optional AI-enhanced explanations.

# --- Reference: the exact variables the code reads ---------------------------
# OPENAI_API_KEY - read via API_KEY_ENV_VAR in src/llm_explainer.py and app.py.
#                  Leave unset (or blank) to run fully offline.
# OPENAI_MODEL   - read via MODEL_ENV_VAR in src/llm_explainer.py.
#                  Leave unset to use the built-in default (gpt-4o-mini).
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_MODEL=
